data.content:
For your `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, and `Directory.Exists` calls, the best approach is the **[System.IO.Abstractions](https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.IO.Abstractions)** NuGet package — a battle-tested library that wraps the entire `System.IO` surface.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, "content");

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/tmp/reports", new MockDirectoryData() }
});

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
generator.GenerateReport("sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists("/tmp/reports/sales.txt"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` already covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, and more — no need to maintain your own interface. The `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem for tests with zero custom mocking code.

> **Note:** Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `DateTime.UtcNow` and `Environment.*`. For those, inject `TimeProvider` (built-in on .NET 10) and a custom `IEnvironmentProvider` respectively. Want me to set those up too?
